  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure will be in control for much of the upcoming week with \nlittle in the way of prospects for rain. Tonight will again see good \nradiational cooling but is not expected to be quite as chilly as \ndewpoints are a bit higher, putting lows in the 20s and 30s. Monday \nwill see milder temperatures in the upper 50s to low 60s with \nincreasing high clouds from the distant nor'easter off the coast of \nthe Carolinas, and these may be enough to limit cooling Monday \nnight, especially over eastern and southern areas. Some increase in \nmoisture may result in low stratus or fog along the coast and \ninterior Monday night but is uncertain due to the clouds and maybe \nenough of a breeze to keep things mixed. Otherwise, similar highs \ncontinue Tuesday with more sunshine by the afternoon, and then \ntemperatures cool down a bit into the mid 50s for highs on \nWednesday. \n\nThe first very low chance of rain comes Thursday as a moisture-\nstarved front crosses, and the next is Thursday night or Friday as \nan upper trough swings through, which will also keep the\nnor'easter well to the south and east, although it still may be\na bit breezy near the coast. Only a handful of ensemble members\nbring measurable precip whether from the front, upper trough, or\neven enough moisture from the distant nor'easter, and the \nlatest NBM PoP of only around 10% reflects this. High pressure \nthen returns for at least the first half of the weekend \nprolonging the mostly dry weather. High temperatures trend back \nup to near or slightly above normal on Thursday ahead of the \nfront and then return to slightly below normal late week and the\nstart of the weekend. \n\nThe dry air in place much of this week will also favor slightly \ncooler than normal low temperatures, mostly in the 20s and 30s.",
